#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Benchmark Campfire implementations side by side as bare processes on Linux (no Docker for the apps).

  bench/setup-native                                        # once: build all three apps + bench/loadgen
  bench/run-native [--apps reference,elixir-official,ours] [--reps 3] [--out DIR] [--user-agent UA]
  bench/report DIR

The Linux counterpart of bench/run, closer to once-campfire-rust's bench/run: same seed, same suites
(bench/lib/common.py), but each app runs natively as its production process model, pinned with
taskset to SERVER_CPUS, while bench/loadgen runs pinned to LOADGEN_CPUS (disjoint physical cores)
over loopback. Each rep, for each app in turn (never two at once; the order alternates between
reps): a fresh copy of bench/seed in a run dir, the app started from nothing (cold start = until
GET /up answers 200), idle memory after SETTLE_SECS, then the HTTP, Action Cable fan-out and upload
suites, with the app's memory sampled every 250 ms throughout. Then its whole process tree (its
session) is killed.

Apps (see bench/README.md, "Native Linux"):
  reference        Rails at REFERENCE_DIR: `bundle exec thrust bin/start-app` (Thruster on PORT,
                   Puma on PORT+1) + `resque-pool`, as its Procfile/bin/boot, jemalloc preloaded.
                   Rails.root/storage is a symlink to the run dir.
  elixir-official  basecamp/once-campfire-elixir's release at ELIXIR_OFFICIAL_DIR behind Thruster,
                   as its bin/container-start (helpers campfire-vips/campfire-html on PATH).
  ours             this repo's release (`_build/prod/rel/campfire`), serving PORT itself.
Redis is NOT part of any app's process tree here: the reference and the official port both use the
external one at REDIS_URL (a Docker container on the host), FLUSHALLed before every run; its memory
is recorded separately. In the Docker images it runs inside the container.

Memory is the sum over the app's processes (its session: workers, Thruster, resque, helpers) of
Pss and Rss from /proc/PID/smaps_rollup, in MB: `idle_pss_mb` after settling, `peak_pss_mb` the
maximum of the 250 ms samples over the whole run (no page cache, unlike the Docker cgroup numbers).

Knobs (env): SERVER_CPUS=4-7 LOADGEN_CPUS=8-11 HARNESS_CPUS=0-3 (this script, the memory sampler)
  HTTP_SECS=8 HTTP_CONCS="1 16 64" CABLE_CLIENTS="100 500 1000" CABLE_TPUT_SECS=15 CABLE_POSTERS=4
  UPLOAD_REPS=5 PORT=4390 (and PORT+1) SUITES="http cable upload" SETTLE_SECS=10 LOAD_MAX=3
  LOAD_WAIT_SECS=300 REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0 EXTRA_ENV="K=V ..." (every app)
  REFERENCE_DIR, ELIXIR_OFFICIAL_DIR (default bench/.cache/once-campfire{,-elixir}), OURS_REL (default
  _build/prod/rel/campfire, e.g. a `mix release --path` build of another commit for A/B), RUBY_VERSION
  (default: REFERENCE_DIR/.ruby-version, run through `mise exec ruby@...`), JEMALLOC=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libjemalloc.so.2
"""
import argparse, glob, json, os, resource, shutil, signal, socket, subprocess, sys, threading, time, urllib.request
from datetime import datetime

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "lib"))
from common import (BENCH, SEED, LOADGEN, E, HTTP_SECS, HTTP_CONCS, CABLE_CLIENTS, PORT, SUITES, SETTLE_SECS,
                    EXTRA_ENV, BASE, log, cpuset_size, app_env, neuter_deliveries, run_suites, load_labels, write_report)

